  "about": "I've spent the last five years at Charter Communications working my way from a summer intern up to Software Engineer V, mostly by chasing the hard problems other people would rather avoid. These days my work lives at the interception of systems architecture and AI infrastructure, where I design the kind of backend foundations that help large engineering organizations move faster without things breaking underneath them. I care deeply about distributed systems, clean API design, and the quiet architectural choices that decide whether a team can actually scale. If a project involves taking something tangled and making it feel simple, that's usually where you'll find me.\n \nA few things I'm proud of. I led the design and delivery of my organization's first production system of its kind for AI-driven access to enterprise tools, and it grew from a prototype to serving well over a thousand users within weeks of launch. Before that, I built an internal integration that replaced a costly third-party product and saved the company tens of thousands of dollars a year. I've also rescued a high-stakes data migration under a punishing deadline without losing a single record. More than once I've owned the architecture and mentored senior engineers on projects well before my title said I should, which taught me that impact travels faster than job levels.\n \nWhen I step away from the keyboard, I'm usually out on a trail somewhere, under a barbell, or tracking down a really good cup of coffee. I've always believed the best engineers stay curious far outside their day jobs, and those three keep me grounded.",
